# Obsidian and its plugins rewrite these JSON files in full whenever a setting
# changes or a new property appears — clipping a note is enough to rewrite
# types.json. With obsidian-git auto-committing that on one machine and the same
# file edited on another, every pull collides on machine-local state nobody is
# actually trying to merge.
#
# `merge=ours` keeps the local copy on a conflicting merge instead of stopping to
# ask. That is what you want here: Obsidian regenerates this state anyway, and a
# fresh clone still gets the committed version because a first checkout is not a
# merge.
#
# Enable the driver once per clone (it cannot live in the repo):
#
#     git config merge.ours.driver true
#
# Without that config Git falls back to a normal conflict, so this degrades
# safely rather than breaking.

.obsidian/*.json                merge=ours
.obsidian/plugins/*/data.json   merge=ours

# Keep real content merging normally — notes, templates, scripts and bases are
# edited deliberately and a conflict there is worth seeing.
